<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mōri Terumoto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1553">1553</a>–<a href="/years/1625">1625</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mori Terumoto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mōri Terumoto (1553–1625) was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> who ruled much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>, which he founded. One of the five regents appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, he was the nominal commander of the western army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600, though he stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>. After the defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> reduced his lands to the provinces of Suō and Nagato, which became the Chōshū domain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto.jpg" alt="Mōri Terumoto" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mori_Terumoto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
